<div class="hero">
  <h1>Netops</h1>
  <p class="hero-lede">
    A small network-design demo: one model, <code>NetworkHost</code>, and the
    full table experience — filtering, column sorting and pagination — built
    with plain Rails and <a href="https://turbo.hotwired.dev">Turbo Frames</a>.
  </p>
  <p class="hero-actions">
    <%= link_to "Browse the host inventory", network_hosts_path, class: "button" %>
    <%= link_to "Add a host", new_network_host_path, class: "button button-quiet" %>
  </p>
</div>

<div class="feature-grid">
  <div class="feature">
    <h2>Turbo Frames</h2>
    <p>
      The index table lives inside a <code>&lt;turbo-frame&gt;</code>. Sorting,
      filtering, pagination, row deletes and in-row status changes all replace
      just the frame — no full-page reloads, no JavaScript.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <h2>Turbo Streams</h2>
    <p>
      Actions that originate from inside the frame answer with
      <code>turbo_stream</code> responses: the changed row is updated or
      removed in place and the flash message streams in.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <h2>Reusable concerns</h2>
    <p>
      <code>Filterable</code> and <code>Sortable</code> model concerns,
      <code>Paginatable</code> and <code>TurboFlash</code> controller
      concerns, plus shared filter/pagination partials — any future model
      (Locations, Vlans, …) gets the same table for free.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
